    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d497">

                <pb facs="0575" n="473" xml:id="pg473"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/12542: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Canton (Ballantine) to the
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Canton</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-11-28T10:00:00+08:00">November 28, 1933—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 28—3 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>My November 18, 5 p.m.<anchor n="97" xml:id="d497-f1"/> Marshal Chen informed me
                    yesterday that delegates who had arrived from Fukien to urge Canton to join
                    movement were told that Canton could not do this as the Fukien leaders were
                    cooperating with the Communists and Japanese. Public manifestoes are of a
                    similar tenor but harp also on Fukien’s repudiation of the Kuomintang.</p>
                <p>Public uneasiness appears subsiding.</p>
                <p>Repeated to the Department, the Legation and to Nanking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Ballantine</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
